{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NSWEnXbPdkxseJXSe4k1YV","type":"tool_result","content":"294\t/// Header-protection key.  All QUIC cipher suites use a single AES\n295\t/// (or ChaCha20) ECB-style transform: feed it a 16-byte sample of\n296\t/// ciphertext and it returns a 16-byte mask whose first 5 bytes\n297\t/// XOR into the protected header.  The trait surface here is\n298\t/// exactly that bare cipher op — picoquic-side logic\n299\t/// ([`crate::header_protection`]) handles sampling, slicing the\n300\t/// header, and applying the mask.\n301\tpub trait HeaderKey: Send {\n302\t    /// Apply the ECB-style block to `sample`, producing the 16-byte\n303\t    /// header-protection mask.  RFC 9001 §5.4.\n304\t    fn mask(&self, sample: [u8; 16]) -> [u8; 16];\n305\t}\n306\t\n307\t/// Bundle of keys associated with a single epoch.\n308\tpub struct Keys {\n309\t    pub header: KeyPairHeader,\n310\t    pub packet: KeyPair,\n311\t}\n312\t\n313\t/// Pair of header-protection keys.\n314\tpub struct KeyPairHeader {\n315\t    pub local: Box<dyn HeaderKey>,\n316\t    pub remote: Box<dyn HeaderKey>,\n317\t}\n318\t\n319\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n320\t// Side-channel data surfaced through `Session`.\n321\t\n322\t/// Server-side handshake data.  Available after the first\n323\t/// ClientHello arrives.\n324\tpub struct HandshakeData {\n325\t    /// SNI (server-name-indication) value from the ClientHello.\n326\t    pub sni: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n327\t    /// Negotiated ALPN value, if any.\n328\t    pub alpn: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n329\t}\n330\t\n331\t/// Peer identity (typically the certificate chain).\n332\tpub enum PeerIdentity {\n333\t    /// X.509 certificate chain.  Each element is one DER-encoded\n334\t    /// certificate.\n335\t    Certificates(Vec<Vec<u8>>),\n336\t}\n337\t\n338\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n339\t// Application-supplied callbacks.\n340\t\n341\t/// Hooks the TLS layer invokes on the application.  One trait\n342\t/// covers ALPN selection, the resumption-ticket store, and\n343\t/// certificate verification.  Default implementations cover\n344\t/// the simple cases; override for custom behavior.\n345\t///\n346\t/// The application installs an implementation at QUIC-context\n347\t/// construction time (one entry point — no C-style global\n348\t/// registry of function pointers).\n349\tpub trait TlsCallbacks: Send {\n350\t    /// Negotiate ALPN from the client's proposal list.  Default:\n351\t    /// pick the first proposal that matches the server's\n352\t    /// configured ALPN list (set on the backend's config).\n353\t    fn select_alpn(&mut self, _list: &[&[u8]]) -> Option<usize> {\n354\t        None\n355\t    }\n356\t\n357\t    /// Server-side: look up a previously-issued session ticket\n358\t    /// keyed by the SNI / ALPN combination.  Default: return\n359\t    /// `None` (no resumption).\n360\t    fn lookup_ticket(&mut self, _sni: &str, _alpn: &str) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {\n361\t        None\n362\t    }\n363\t\n364\t    /// Server-side: persist a freshly-issued session ticket.\n365\t    /// Default: discard.\n366\t    fn store_ticket(&mut self, _sni: &str, _alpn: &str, _ticket: &[u8]) {}\n367\t\n368\t    /// Verify the peer certificate chain.  Default: trust any\n369\t    /// chain rooted in the system trust store (backends that\n370\t    /// don't have a system store provide their own default).\n371\t    fn verify_certificate(&mut self, _certs: &[&[u8]]) -> Result<(), Error> {\n372\t        Ok(())\n373\t    }"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d295fe5c-73e0-4b20-9131-8117f7409d00","uuid":"39ca7df6-7667-490b-90ab-b9700502cb51","timestamp":"2026-05-07T09:55:30.163Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls.rs","content":"/// Header-protection key.  All QUIC cipher suites use a single AES\n/// (or ChaCha20) ECB-style transform: feed it a 16-byte sample of\n/// ciphertext and it returns a 16-byte mask whose first 5 bytes\n/// XOR into the protected header.  The trait surface here is\n/// exactly that bare cipher op — picoquic-side logic\n/// ([`crate::header_protection`]) handles sampling, slicing the\n/// header, and applying the mask.\npub trait HeaderKey: Send {\n    /// Apply the ECB-style block to `sample`, producing the 16-byte\n    /// header-protection mask.  RFC 9001 §5.4.\n    fn mask(&self, sample: [u8; 16]) -> [u8; 16];\n}\n\n/// Bundle of keys associated with a single epoch.\npub struct Keys {\n    pub header: KeyPairHeader,\n    pub packet: KeyPair,\n}\n\n/// Pair of header-protection keys.\npub struct KeyPairHeader {\n    pub local: Box<dyn HeaderKey>,\n    pub remote: Box<dyn HeaderKey>,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Side-channel data surfaced through `Session`.\n\n/// Server-side handshake data.  Available after the first\n/// ClientHello arrives.\npub struct HandshakeData {\n    /// SNI (server-name-indication) value from the ClientHello.\n    pub sni: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n    /// Negotiated ALPN value, if any.\n    pub alpn: Option<Vec<u8>>,\n}\n\n/// Peer identity (typically the certificate chain).\npub enum PeerIdentity {\n    /// X.509 certificate chain.  Each element is one DER-encoded\n    /// certificate.\n    Certificates(Vec<Vec<u8>>),\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Application-supplied callbacks.\n\n/// Hooks the TLS layer invokes on the application.  One trait\n/// covers ALPN selection, the resumption-ticket store, and\n/// certificate verification.  Default implementations cover\n/// the simple cases; override for custom behavior.\n///\n/// The application installs an implementation at QUIC-context\n/// construction time (one entry point — no C-style global\n/// registry of function pointers).\npub trait TlsCallbacks: Send {\n    /// Negotiate ALPN from the client's proposal list.  Default:\n    /// pick the first proposal that matches the server's\n    /// configured ALPN list (set on the backend's config).\n    fn select_alpn(&mut self, _list: &[&[u8]]) -> Option<usize> {\n        None\n    }\n\n    /// Server-side: look up a previously-issued session ticket\n    /// keyed by the SNI / ALPN combination.  Default: return\n    /// `None` (no resumption).\n    fn lookup_ticket(&mut self, _sni: &str, _alpn: &str) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {\n        None\n    }\n\n    /// Server-side: persist a freshly-issued session ticket.\n    /// Default: discard.\n    fn store_ticket(&mut self, _sni: &str, _alpn: &str, _ticket: &[u8]) {}\n\n    /// Verify the peer certificate chain.  Default: trust any\n    /// chain rooted in the system trust store (backends that\n    /// don't have a system store provide their own default).\n    fn verify_certificate(&mut self, _certs: &[&[u8]]) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        Ok(())\n    }","numLines":80,"startLine":294,"totalLines":390}}}
